"[2412.03212] Semi-Supervised Transfer Boosting (SS-TrBoosting) (https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.03212)\nciteturn96view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.03212\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 162\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 4 Dec 2024]\nL9: # Title:Semi-Supervised Transfer Boosting (SS-TrBoosting)\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Lingfei Deng , cite6†Changming Zhao , cite7†Zhenbang Du , cite8†Kun Xia , cite9†Dongrui Wu L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Semi-Supervised Transfer Boosting (SS-TrBoosting), by Lingfei Deng and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Semi-supervised domain adaptation (SSDA) aims at training a high-performance model for a target domain using few labeled target data, many unlabeled target data, and plenty of auxiliary data from a source domain. Previous works in SSDA mainly focused on learning transferable representations across domains. However, it is difficult to find a feature space where the source and target domains share the same conditional probability distribution.\nL17: Additionally, there is no flexible and effective strategy extending existing unsupervised domain adaptation (UDA) approaches to SSDA settings. In order to solve the above two challenges, we propose a novel fine-tuning framework, semi-supervised transfer boosting (SS-TrBoosting). Given a well-trained deep learning-based UDA or SSDA model, we use it as the initial model, generate additional base learners by boosting, and then use all of them as an ensemble.\nL18: More specifically, half of the base learners are generated by supervised domain adaptation, and half by semi-supervised learning. Furthermore, for more efficient data transmission and better data privacy protection, we propose a source data generation approach to extend SS-TrBoosting to semi-supervised source-free domain adaptation (SS-SFDA). Extensive experiments showed that SS-TrBoosting can be applied to a variety of existing UDA, SSDA and SFDA approaches to further improve their performance.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2412.03212 [cs.CV]\nL21:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2412.03212v1 [cs.CV] for this version)\nL22:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.03212†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: Journal reference:  | IEEE Trans. on Artificial Intelligence, 5(7):3431-3444, 2024\nL24: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | cite15†https://doi.org/10.1109/TAI.2024.3350543†doi.org DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Dongrui Wu [cite16†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Wed, 4 Dec 2024 10:57:55 UTC (2,430 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Semi-Supervised Transfer Boosting (SS-TrBoosting), by Lingfei Deng and 3 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite10†View PDF L37:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite17†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite18†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.CV\nL44: \nL45: cite19†< prev |   cite20†next > L46: \nL47: cite21†new | cite22†recent | cite23†2024-12 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite24†cs L52: cite25†cs.LG L53: \nL54: ### References & Citations\nL55: \nL56:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L57:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L58:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L59: \nL60: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading..