s/efficient-structure-slimming-for-spiking-neural-networks/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Skip to main navigation cite1†Skip to search cite2†Skip to main content L1: \nL2: cite3†University of Birmingham Home L3: \nL4:   * cite4†Help & FAQ   Link opens in a new tab†intranet.birmingham.ac.uk L5: \nL6: Search content at University of Birmingham [Input: Search...]\nL7: # Efficient Structure Slimming for Spiking Neural Networks\nL8: \nL9:   * Yaxin Li\nL10:   * , Xuanye Fang\nL11:   * , Yuyuan Gao\nL12:   * , Dongdong Zhou\nL13:   * , Jiangrong Shen\nL14:   * , cite5†Jian K. Liu L15:   * , Gang Pan\nL16:   * , Qi Xu^{*}\nL17: \nL18: ^{*}Corresponding author for this work\nL19: \nL20:   * cite6†Computer Science L21: \nL22: Research output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\nL23: \nL24: 475 Downloads (Pure)\nL25: ## Abstract\nL26: Spiking neural networks (SNNs) are deeply inspired by biological neural information systems. Compared to convolutional neural networks (CNNs), SNNs are low power consumption because of their spike based information processing mechanism. However, most of the current structures of SNNs are fully-connected or converted from deep CNNs which poses redundancy connections. While the structure and topology in human brain systems are sparse and efficient.\nL27: This paper aims at taking full advantage of sparse structure and low power consumption which lie in human brain and proposed efficient structure slimming methods. Inspired by the development of biological neural network structures, this paper designed types of structure slimming methods including neuron pruning and channel pruning. In addition to pruning, this paper also considers the growth and development of the nervous system.\nL28: Through iterative application of the proposed neural pruning and rewiring algorithms, experimental evaluations on CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and DVS-Gesture datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of the structure slimming methods. When the parameter count is reduced to only about 10% of the original, the performance decreases by less than 1%.\nL29: Original language  | English\nL30: Article number  | 10391076\nL31: Number of pages  | 9\nL32: Journal  | cite7†IEEE Transactions on Artificial Intelligence L33: Early online date  | 11 Jan 2024\nL34: DOIs  |\nL35: \nL36:   * cite8†https://doi.org/10.1109/tai.2024.3352533†doi.org L37: Publication status  | E-pub ahead of print - 11 Jan 2024\nL38: ### Bibliographical note\nL39: \nL40: Funding:\nL41: This work is supported by National Natural Science Foundation of China (NSFC No.62206037, 62306274), The Open Project of Anhui Provincial Key Laboratory of Multimodal Cognitive Computation, Anhui University, No. MMC202104, Open Research Fund from Guangdong Laboratory of Artificial Intelligence and Digital Economy (SZ), under Grant No. GMLKF-22-11 and the Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities (DUT21RC(3)091)\nL42: ## Access to Document\nL43: \nL44:   * cite8†10.1109/tai.2024.3352533†doi.org Licence: None: All rights reserved\nL45: \nL46:   * cite9†LiY2024Efficient L47: This is the accepted author manuscript of an article published in IEEE Transactions on Artificial Intelligence; final published version available at https://doi.org/10.1109/TAI.2024.3352533 © 2024 IEEE. Personal use of this material is permitted.\nL48: Permission from IEEE must be obtained for all other uses, in any current or future media, including reprinting/republishing this material for advertising or promotional purposes, creating new collective works, for resale or redistribution to servers or lists, or reuse of any copyrighted component of this work in other works.\nL49: Accepted author manuscript, 1.63 MB Licence: cite10†Other (please specify with Rights Statement) L50: ## Fingerprint\nL51: \nL52: Dive into the research topics of 'Efficient Structure Slimming for Spiking Neural Networks'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\nL53: \n